n the new species the gena is shorter and less rounded posterior to eyes (Fig. 64), and ovipositor is with distinct dorsal subapical teeth (Fig. 66). Tersilochus serratoides sp. nov. also differs from the occurring in China T. ningxiator Khalaim & Sheng (Khalaim & Sheng 2009) by completely lacking foveate groove of the mesopleuron (Fig. 67) and longer ovipositor (Fig. 61), and from T. runatus Khalaim & Sheng (Khalaim & Sheng 2009) by shorter gena, flagellum with 18 flagellomeres (15 – 16 in T. runatus) and more slender ovipositor (Fig. 61).","","text","",""]]},"TZ5VH":{"d":[["SE-Brazil (Minas Gerais)","","text","",""]]},"TZT8G":{"d":[["KOR","KR","iso","",""],["JAP-HN","JAP-HN","tdwg","",""],["JAP-KY","JAP-KY","tdwg","",""],["Hokkaido","","text","",""],["Tsushima","","text","",""]]},"ZVKF":{"d":[["New Guinea (Papua New Guinea)","","text","",""]]}}